2.1. Why Make a Myth

i. 	To persecute myths further, you must ask beyond what they are; what is key for 
any community is to ask why they are. At the simplest level, myths exist because other 
myths exist; the concept of a myth has developed over time since the start of Roblox 
itself. It is unlikely that people just think up what a myth is and do it.3 A myth is a result 
of inspiration from any source; it is a creative expression that results from what others 
have expressed. A lot of people want to be a myth because it is a way to a spotlight, 
notably with its lack of large need for huge social interaction; it provides a way for your 
art to be seen and engaged with more than other forms of art, relative to community 
size. Outwardly, people get to see your perspectives and experiences, they get to enjoy 
your art, and form a community. Inwardly, you receive that satisfaction that your work 
has reached people, that people enjoy you, and you get the feeling of being heard. Aside 
from the joys of community, there is also the competitive side to myths, between others, 
maybe for visits, attention, hunts, and so on. A myth is created because one feels the 
need to build a character and a game as their form of creative expression, whether that 
feeling of expression is fulfilled by others or by your own satisfaction, or by both.

ii. 	How much does one really receive as a myth? Myths don’t appear out of nowhere, 
either; you have to build up skills to make progress. Before your skills are developed, the 
community may put you down. It can seem too daunting a work to express oneself in, so 
many end up seeking shortcuts. What are these shortcuts for, though? Is there some 
grand level you reach where all of that time and effort has gotten you fame that can 
match it? In the modern myth community, there is nothing up there. Your passion over 
the years leads up to a few friends that you keep around, maybe, or perhaps a few 
YouTube videos and high ranks in groups. Does that all matter, though? This may be to 
then see some newer or less passionate myth surpass your connection in the community, 
purely on determination, though lacking “real” effort.4 This is not a matter of taking 
those determined down, though; this is more a matter of uplifting everyone. This 
community has let the skilled down and ignored the unskilled; in a community like this, 
there should not be such divisive conversation. Going further, is there legacy in 
consideration when making a myth? Your influence inspires hundreds, maybe your 
name gets remembered for a couple of months to years, but there is seemingly no height 
of influence for myths nowadays, at least where we have any lasting effect. We are going 
nowhere, but that is to be discussed later on.

