2.2. Why Hunt Myths

i. 	Though it is not my best area to speak on,5 I do believe that everybody in the 
community is a hunter in some way. I doubt anyone becomes a myth and leaves hunting 
behind; it is a big way to compare, to build up your own craft, and it can form 
friendships. As a hunter, you enjoy myths as anyone else would enjoy a story game for 
example; it is simply something you engage with for fun. Going deeper, a hunter can 
earn some satisfaction with completion, or in making large progress in more difficult 
myths, a hunter can find a place working with others, or the expansion of their context of 
the world as they receive unique ideas and experiences. Like art, it is all about hearing 
the expression of others, what they can grant you, how they can speak about the world 
in ways which you could never think of, and to me, it is all about the market of 
perspective, from one mind to another, bought with time and effort.

ii. 	Community-wise, there are many paths and benefits to be had through groups, 
interactions, and upholding the community. Hunters are what myths live on; they are 
the basic community members, but they are not fodder either. Hunting grants you 
influence, and if you do enough for a group, you earn the satisfaction of making an impact 
in a small shard of the community. As a hunter, you’re not only hunting myths as your 
passion, but taking up high roles, guiding groups you’re in, or forming groups. This 
upholding of the community is a legacy that myths can not match, as we generally don’t 
take important ranks in groups. Hunters are what facilitate all of the attention for myths, 
sometimes using that power to overrepresent their own myths, though that’s for later as 
well. The most well-known hunters form groups, museums, and other places, which then 
leave a lasting remnant of their influence for years to come. Overall, there is everything 
to gain in becoming a devoted hunter; there is no creative wall hit, only difficult group 
interactions, drama, or larger conflicts. Avoiding those can vary in difficulty, but the 
main point is that hunters are everything.

