2.3. Why Engage in Art

i. 	Connecting to why people engage in the community, hunter or myth, is why 
people engage with art in general. As I have said, art is an exchange of perspective; art is 
one person utilising their ability to express themselves and their perspectives for others 
to observe and apply their own minds to. Art is not only an exchange of perspective, but 
an exchange of experience and feeling as well. To the cave dweller, expanding horizons 
beyond the cave means engaging in outside ideas, experiences, and feelings, which all 
grant the cave dweller an easier path forward in life, a greater idea of possibilities. Art 
enriches your life in many unseen ways, from what you see in your future, to how you 
relate to others, to working life, and your ability to problem solve. The expansion of the 
mind is beneficial for your future, for your present actions, and for your perspectives on 
the past. Finding a community in art is also important, not only for this thought 
exchange, but for people you can relate with or a culture you can be a part of. 
Community is the most important aspect; nobody engages in their hobbies or interests 
without a community to support them, to relate to. Everybody needs a community; 
humans are social by nature, and the instinct to fit in is deeply human, rooted in 
evolution, where it often meant the difference between life and death.

ii. 	The reason to make art is the same as making a myth; it is a quick way to garner 
attention, the effort in your craft is visible, and it can convey a wide variety of ideas and 
experiences. What is beyond myths is the size of the community and what follows from 
popularity. For artists in more popular or established communities, much more 
recognition and monetary gain can form. People can have their art take the place of a job, 
even full-time, a luxury not one individual in the myth community could achieve. Art 
culture surpasses the art itself, and enters other forms such as videos, written analysis, 
and other commentary. This all ends up contributing to the exchange of perspectives 
and ideas that form the central basis of engagement with art; there is this inherent will 
to have others see your effort when you finish a project, you want to share the effort, 
time, and thought that went into the formation of this creative expression.


